Package: magpie
Version: @VERSION@
Architecture: @ARCH@
Maintainer: Matthew Hagrelius <matthew@hagreli.us>
Installed-Size: @INSTALLED_SIZE@
Depends: @DEPENDS@, yt-dlp
Recommends: ffmpeg
Section: video
Priority: optional
Homepage: https://github.com/mhagrelius/magpie
Description: Bring videos home from the web
 Magpie downloads video and audio from the web for the GNOME desktop. Paste a
 link, choose a quality, and the file lands in your Downloads folder with a
 record of where it came from.
 .
 Quality presets reach past 360p by merging the separate video and audio streams
 that modern sites serve, and audio-only downloads can be kept in their original
 format or converted to MP3 or M4A. Playlists and channels arrive as a list of
 items to pick from, in a folder of their own.
 .
 The queue survives closing the window and resumes a part-finished download
 rather than starting it again. Downloads can be paused, resumed and cancelled,
 and a failure names a cause and a remedy instead of relaying a stack trace.
 .
 Downloading is done by yt-dlp, which is a dependency rather than something
 Magpie bundles or downloads for itself. Note that the yt-dlp in a stable Debian
 or Ubuntu release is often too old to work against YouTube; if downloads fail,
 the Tools page in Preferences shows the installed version and its age.
 .
 Transcripts are optional and need whisper.cpp, for which Ubuntu has no package.
 Build this .deb with --with-whisper to compile and include one.
